* text=auto eol=lf

# Vixen source and asset text formats
*.cs          text eol=lf diff=csharp
*.csproj      text eol=lf
*.slnx        text eol=lf
*.props       text eol=lf
*.targets     text eol=lf
*.rvn         text eol=lf
*.vxml        text eol=lf
*.vcss        text eol=lf
*.g4          text eol=lf
*.meta        text eol=lf
*.vxasset     text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.json        text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf

# Golden/snapshot fixtures must never be normalised
*.tree        text eol=lf -diff
*.approved.*  text eol=lf -diff
# The wire corpus is what "bit-exact" is asserted against, so a checkout that rewrote a byte of it
# would make the gate assert something other than what was committed. LF like everything else, and
# diffable, because reading the diff is the whole point of it being hex rather than a hash.
*.txt         text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts
*.cmd         text eol=crlf
*.bat         text eol=crlf
*.ps1         text eol=crlf

# Binary
# .bin first, because it is the one that would have gone wrong quietly: the fuzz corpus in
# Core/Vixen.Fuzz.Tests is packets that crashed a decoder, and `text=auto` classifies a file
# without a NUL byte in it as text. A crasher that happened to contain CR LF would then be rewritten
# on a Windows checkout into a different packet — one that no longer reproduces, in a directory whose
# entire purpose is that these reproduce.
*.bin  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ktx2 binary
*.dds  binary
*.fbx  binary
*.gltf binary
*.glb  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.wasm binary
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary

# The engine's own binary asset formats, for .bin's reason and with a sharper edge on it: these are
# packed little-endian floats and ushorts, so whether a given file happens to contain a NUL byte in
# the range git samples is a property of the terrain somebody sculpted. Outskirts.vxterrain has 29
# NUL bytes in the 8 KB git looks at — a heightfield with no zero-valued sample, which is what a
# terrain sitting entirely above its own base height is, would have none at all, be classified text,
# and be rewritten on a Windows checkout. Its samples are not lines, and a CR LF inside one is
# two height values, so the rewrite shifts every byte after it. The importers then reject their own
# magic number (VXFL, VXTERRA1) if they are lucky, and import shifted instance data if they are not.
# Which of the two you get is decided by where in the file the pair fell.
*.vxfol     binary
*.vxterrain binary
# HeightmapImporter's two: a .r16 is width × height × two bytes with no header at all — there is not
# even a magic number to refuse with, so a rewritten one imports silently as a terrain of vertical
# noise. A .hmpng is a 16-bit PNG, read by TerrainHeightmapPng rather than the texture path, and
# .png's own rule does not reach it because the extension is different.
*.r16       binary
*.hmpng     binary

# The source formats Vixen.Editor.Assets claims and reads as bytes rather than through a
# StreamReader. Same hazard, no engine authorship: these arrive from a DCC and go straight into a
# decoder. jump.wav already carries a CR LF pair and is saved only by the 68 NUL bytes beside it.
# The text source formats the importers also claim — .obj and .dae, and the .cube LUT that
# CubeLutImporter reads through a StreamReader — are deliberately left to `text=auto`.
*.jpeg  binary
*.bmp   binary
*.tga   binary
*.psd   binary
*.gif   binary
*.hdr   binary
*.wav   binary
*.wave  binary
*.ogg   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.flac  binary
# .opus has no importer — it is the voice codec, read by OpusStreamDecoder — but its fixtures in
# Core/Vixen.Audio.Codecs.Tests are committed, and one of the assertions on them is about the file's
# length. A rewrite would change the number the test compares against, which is not a failure that
# reads as an encoding problem.
*.opus  binary
*.webm  binary
*.mkv   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.m4v   binary
*.mov   binary
*.3ds   binary
*.blend binary
# .ply and .stl each have an ASCII form and a binary form under one extension. Declared binary
# because that is the form a rewrite destroys; an ASCII one merely goes un-normalised, which costs
# nothing.
*.ply   binary
*.stl   binary

# Two more that no importer claims but that are committed and were falling to `text=auto`.
# docs/api-history's Brotli is the thinnest margin in the repository: 2.5 MB of entropy-coded stream
# with 33 NUL bytes in the range git samples and 26 CR LF pairs already sitting in it. Those pairs
# are compressed data, not line ends. One regenerated snapshot that happens to place no NUL in the
# first 8 KB is classified text, and a Windows checkout then rewrites 26 bytes of a stream whose
# decoder has no way to tell you which byte went wrong — it just fails to decompress.
*.br    binary
# SPIR-V modules are 32-bit words. The NUL margin here is structural rather than lucky (OpName pads
# its strings to word boundaries), so this is belt-and-braces — but it also stops git offering a
# textual diff of one, which is never what anybody wanted.
*.spv   binary
